                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_495">Shaftesbury,
                            Dorset, Abbey of St Mary the Virgin and St Edward, of Benedictine
                            nuns</orgName> <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of
                            Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                            Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain
                            index card records the following reason for a potential attribution:
                            'Cest livere treita <persName role="fmo">willame giffard</persName> |
                            Chapelein del iglise seint edward...' (at end). See article by J.C. Fox
                            [owner of the MS.], 'An Anglo-Norman Apocalypse from Shaftesbury Abbey',
                            <title>Modern Languages Review</title>, viii (1913), 338-51. The index
                            card also has a later summative comment in Ker's hand: 'Reject: no
                            evidence that this copy belonged to Shaftesbury, even if, as seems
                            likely, W. G. was chaplain there.'</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">? John Atridge,
                            </persName>South Weald (co. Essex) d. 1741 (cf. fol. i).</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Peter Alfred
                            Taylor</persName>, MP for Leicester, late 20th century.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">After Taylor's death, passed to
                            <persName role="fmo">Sir J.C. Fox</persName>, Goring-on-Thames (see
                            above for his article).</provenance>
